testModule(QQ,Ideal) -- computes the test ideal of a pair
*********************************************************

  * Function: *note testModule: (TestIdeals)testModule,
  * Usage: 
        J = testModule(t, I)
  * Inputs:
      * t, a *note rational number: (Macaulay2Doc)QQ,, a rational number
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, an ideal
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note AssumeDomain: (TestIdeals)testModule, => ..., default value
        false,
      * *note CanonicalIdeal: (TestIdeals)testModule, => ..., default value
        null,
      * *note CurrentRing: (TestIdeals)testModule, => ..., default value null, 
      * *note FrobeniusRootStrategy: (TestIdeals)testModule, => ..., default
        value Substitution,
      * *note GeneratorList: (TestIdeals)testModule, => ..., default value
        null,
  * Outputs:
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list with the test module and
        the canonical module

Description
===========

This computes the test module $\tau(\omega_R, I^t)$ of an ideal $I$ in a domain
$R$ of index not divisible by the characteristic $p > 0$. It returns a list
with two entries.  The first is the ideal of $R$ isomorphic viewed as an ideal
of $R$ isomorphic to $\tau(\omega_R, J^t)$. The second is an ideal $R$
isomorphic to $\omega_R$, in which the first module sits as a subideal.

We begin with example in a regular ring.

+----------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/5[x,y];         |
+----------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(x^2, y^3);   |
|                            |
|o2 : Ideal of R             |
+----------------------------+
|i3 : testModule(5/6, I)     |
|                            |
|o3 = (ideal (y, x), ideal 1)|
|                            |
|o3 : Sequence               |
+----------------------------+
|i4 : testModule(5/6-1/25, I)|
|                            |
|o4 = (ideal 1, ideal 1)     |
|                            |
|o4 : Sequence               |
+----------------------------+

We now include an example in a non-Gorenstein ring

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : T = ZZ/2[a,b,c,d];                             |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : S = ZZ/2[x,y];                                 |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : f = map(S, T, {x^3, x^2*y, x*y^2, y^3});       |
|                                                    |
|o7 : RingMap S <-- T                                |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : R = T/(ker f);                                 |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : m = ideal(a,b,c,d);                            |
|                                                    |
|o9 : Ideal of R                                     |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : testModule(1, m)                              |
|                                                    |
|                                   2                |
|o10 = (ideal (b*d, a*d, a*c, a*b, a ), ideal (b, a))|
|                                                    |
|o10 : Sequence                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : testModule(1-1/16, m)                         |
|                                                    |
|o11 = (ideal (b, a), ideal (b, a))                  |
|                                                    |
|o11 : Sequence                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note testModule: (TestIdeals)testModule, -- find the parameter test module
    of a reduced ring
  * testModule(QQ,Ideal) (missing documentation)
  * *note testModuleMinusEpsilon: testModuleMinusEpsilon, -- computes the
    (parameter) test module of a pair for values arbitrarily close to, but
    below, t
  * *note testIdeal: (TestIdeals)testIdeal, -- compute a test ideal in a
    Q-Gorenstein ring
  * testIdeal(QQ,Ideal) (missing documentation)

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note testModule(QQ,Ideal): testModule_lpQQ_cmIdeal_rp, -- computes the
    test ideal of a pair
  * "testModule(ZZ,Ideal)"
